Root cause:
The bug was caused by the inability of the system to prevent the same configuration type from being added multiple times during the blueprint deployment process. This led to potential conflicts or errors in cluster configuration as the same config type could be set with different values.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
if (mapClusterConfigurations.containsKey(serviceConfigType)) {
  blueprintConfigRequest.addConfigElement(serviceConfigType,
    mapClusterConfigurations.get(serviceConfigType),
    mapClusterAttributes.get(serviceConfigType));
}
```
Reason:
The code checks if a configuration type exists in `mapClusterConfigurations` and adds it to the `blueprintConfigRequest` without any checks to prevent the same configuration type from being added multiple times. This can lead to conflicts or errors if the same configuration type is set with different values during the blueprint deployment process.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
Collection<String> encounteredConfigTypes = new HashSet<String>();
if (encounteredConfigTypes.add(serviceConfigType)) {
```
Reason:
The addition of a `HashSet` called `encounteredConfigTypes` ensures that each configuration type is only added once. The `add` method of the `HashSet` returns `false` if the item already exists, thus preventing duplicate configuration types from being processed and added to the `blueprintConfigRequest`, thereby resolving the issue of potential conflicts in cluster configuration.


